From 9f76e96ed8130120b01b6cb35cd617484a5234d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 03:21:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rugs, curtains and wall decoration - and the gold leaf they
 are made of

Eighteen more dead hotspots closed: 68 -> 50. Rugs in five rooms, curtains in three, and the
Decoration space in the Combat and Dining rooms, all at OSRS's levels, materials and experience.
One deliberate omission: OSRS's torn curtains also want three nails, and a piece here carries two
materials. The planks and the cloth are the ones worth charging for.

GOLD LEAF. poh_stone.inv has had a note at the top saying gold leaf belonged to whichever round
wired the gilded furniture. This is that round - the opulent rug and the gilded decoration are both
made of it - so the Stonemason stocks it, twenty at a time, at OSRS's own 130,000. The price is not
typed in: cost 260,000 through the shop's cost x multiplier / 1000 with ^poh_stone_sell at 500 comes
out at exactly that, the same arithmetic that already gives the marble block its 125,000. 377's own
gold leaf is an unpriced recoloured papyrus from In Search of the Myreque, so this is its own obj on
the same model and the quest's copy stays the quest's.

A RUG IS ONE PIECE OVER A WHOLE RECTANGLE OF FLOOR - 36 tiles in the Skill hall and the Quest hall,
24 in the Bedroom, 16 in the Parlour, 10 in the Chapel, 122 in all. Same problem the combat ring
had, through the same two doors: the "show" hand-off for placement and an "anchor" so the piece is
stored at one tile per room whichever hotspot was clicked. Rugs and rings share the rotation maths
outright - ~poh_ring_tile and ~poh_ring_angle, which are the engine's own zone arithmetic.

THE ANCHOR IS (7,7) AND THAT NUMBER IS NOT FREE. The combat ring anchors at the room's (0,0); a rug
cannot, because (0,0) carries a Statue space hotspot in the Chapel and a rug and a statue wanting
the same furniture slot means one of them silently cannot be built. (7,7) is the only tile in the
zone carrying no hotspot in any of the five rooms with a Rug space - computed, not chosen - and the
battery now checks that rule for EVERY anchored family against the templates rather than for the
combat ring specifically.

WHAT SAID WHICH PIECE GOES WHERE. The rug tiles form a complete rectangle in every room, so corner,
side and middle follow from position - but the rectangle does not say which of the nine art locs is
a corner, and getting that wrong gives a carpet with fringe down the middle. The hotspot ghosts say
it: every Rug space model is 4v/2f (a plain square), 54v/76f (a fringed edge) or 45v/63f (a fringed
L), and THE FIRST TIER'S THREE PIECES ARE THOSE SAME THREE MODELS, vertex for vertex. Rendering them
with ob2render.py first is what turned that up, and genrugs.py refuses to write if geometry and
cache ever stop agreeing.

Group 60 checks all 122 tiles back against the templates - the tile, its angle, and the kind the
rectangle and the ghost both give it - plus the nine-case piece table, the removals, and that gold
leaf is what and only what the two gilded pieces are made of.

Eleven mutations, and one of them found a hole in the check itself: moving ^poh_rug_anchor from 7 to
1 SURVIVED. The anchor lives in two places - furnspec.json where the click reads it and a constant
where removal reads it - and nothing tied them together, so drift stores the rug at one tile and
looks for it at another while both halves stay internally consistent. That check now exists.

rs2check 0 ERROR / 0 CHECK over 1,671 scripts and 459 configs; poh_battery ALL PASS (60 groups);
poh_mutate every mutation caught.

